      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To determine the association of self-report use of metformin and pain intensity."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Survey-based cross sectional."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Primary care in an academic medical center."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS",
            "content": "Three hundred and twenty nine participants with diabetes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "A total of 329 men and women, aged 18-65, completed a phone-based survey. We utilized the Brief Pain Inventory to assess for pain intensity ratings; Leeds Assessment of Neuropathic Symptoms and Signs to screen for neuropathy; and the Personal Health Questionnaire (PHQ8) Depression Scale to assess for depression."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Three hundred and twenty nine diabetics (mean age 54-\u2009±\u20098-year old) completed the study (162 metformin users, 167 nonusers). Compared with non-users, metformin users were used more often [38% vs 20%, P\u2009=\u20090.001]; had lower mean depression scores [6.8 vs 8.3; P\u2009=\u20090.026] and fewer comorbidities [1.5 vs 1.8, P\u2009=\u20090.022]. Adjusting for those three variables, pain scores were not significantly different between groups. In a subset analyses of those with neuropathic pain (n\u2009=\u2009156), there were no differences in pain scores found between groups."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "In a clinic sample of patients with diabetes, the use of metformin at an average dose of 1,432 mg (SD\u2009=\u2009596 mg) was not associated with lower pain scores. Given the anti-nociceptive effects of metformin in the animal models of pain, and the relative safety of metformin, future research should evaluate the effect of the higher dose of metformin as a potential analgesic."
          }
        ]
      },
